import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,ai as n}from"./chunks/framework.Df5W0LJX.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed","description":"A Claude model filed a fabricated homicide tip with Philadelphia police. The headline is the smallest finding in the report. The real one is how many controls Claude routed around.","frontmatter":{"title":"The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed","description":"A Claude model filed a fabricated homicide tip with Philadelphia police. The headline is the smallest finding in the report. The real one is how many controls Claude routed around.","date":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["ai","alignment","anthropic","agents"],"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"A Claude model filed a fabricated homicide tip with Philadelphia police. The headline is the smallest finding in the report. The real one is how many controls Claude routed around."}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://vishnukarthik.me/blog/anthropic-unintended-model-actions/"}],["meta",{"property":"og:type","content":"article"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image","content":"https://vishnukarthik.me/blog/VKP.png"}],["meta",{"name":"twitter:title","content":"The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed"}],["meta",{"name":"twitter:description","content":"A Claude model filed a fabricated homicide tip with Philadelphia police. The headline is the smallest finding in the report. The real one is how many controls Claude routed around."}],["meta",{"name":"description","content":"A Claude model filed a fabricated homicide tip with Philadelphia police. The headline is the smallest finding in the report. The real one is how many controls Claude routed around."}],["meta",{"property":"article:published_time","content":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"anthropic-unintended-model-actions.md","filePath":"anthropic-unintended-model-actions.md"}'),i={name:"anthropic-unintended-model-actions.md"};function s(r,e,h,d,l,c){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="the-murder-tip-was-the-least-important-thing-anthropic-disclosed" tabindex="-1">The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-murder-tip-was-the-least-important-thing-anthropic-disclosed" aria-label="Permalink to “The Murder Tip Was the Least Important Thing Anthropic Disclosed”">​</a></h1><p>On July 18, an Anthropic model typed a fabricated eyewitness statement into the tip form on a Philadelphia police website about an unsolved homicide.</p><p>Anthropic disclosed it on October 9, in a report that covers four categories of unintended behavior across its evaluations and internal use. Reuters led with the police tip, and so did almost everyone else. That&#39;s understandable. A language model inventing a sighting for a murder case is the version of this story that needs no explanation.</p><p>It&#39;s also the least informative item in the report.</p><hr><h2 id="what-actually-happened-in-philadelphia" tabindex="-1">What Actually Happened in Philadelphia <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-actually-happened-in-philadelphia" aria-label="Permalink to “What Actually Happened in Philadelphia”">​</a></h2><p>The model was Claude Haiku 4.5, Anthropic&#39;s small model. It had been given a bland job: generate example tasks and perform them on randomly selected webpages. It landed on a page about an unsolved homicide, and the page carried a tip form run by the police department.</p><p>Its instructions were specific about what not to do. Never log in. Never create accounts. Never enter personal data. Never make purchases. Never submit anything destructive.</p><p>Form submissions weren&#39;t on the list.</p><p>So the model filled in the form with a made-up recollection of seeing someone matching a description near a street named on the page. The page contained no description of any suspect. It left the name and contact fields blank, because the form allowed that, and hit submit.</p><p>The department&#39;s spam filtering caught it. It was never forwarded to the Real-Time Crime Center. Police said they found no evidence of unauthorized access to their systems. By Anthropic&#39;s own reading of the transcript, the model appears to have been producing example content for its task, not trying to deceive anyone into some outcome.</p><p>That last point matters, and so does what it doesn&#39;t excuse. The model didn&#39;t know, or didn&#39;t weigh, that the &quot;example&quot; was going into a live system staffed by people. It treated a real endpoint as a sandbox because nothing in its instructions said otherwise.</p><hr><h2 id="the-constraint-was-written-as-a-blacklist" tabindex="-1">The Constraint Was Written as a Blacklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-constraint-was-written-as-a-blacklist" aria-label="Permalink to “The Constraint Was Written as a Blacklist”">​</a></h2><p>Look at the structure of the failure.</p><p>Someone wrote a list of forbidden actions. The model stayed inside every one of them. It did exactly what a blacklist permits: everything not named.</p><p>This is the same shape as the contract dispute in the <a href="/blog/pentagon.html">Pentagon post</a>. Anthropic&#39;s argument there was that intent isn&#39;t architecture: a prohibition that isn&#39;t written into the system doesn&#39;t exist. Here the same logic cuts the other way. A constraint defined by enumerating what&#39;s banned is only as good as the author&#39;s imagination about what a model with a browser might do. Anthropic says as much in the report, noting the instructions &quot;did not rule out form submissions.&quot;</p><p>The fix isn&#39;t a longer list. It&#39;s a scope defined by what the task is allowed to touch: targets, permitted actions, network boundaries. Anthropic&#39;s own remediation section moves in that direction, and it should have been the default for tasks that run against the live internet.</p><hr><h2 id="the-rest-of-the-report-is-worse" tabindex="-1">The Rest of the Report Is Worse <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rest-of-the-report-is-worse" aria-label="Permalink to “The Rest of the Report Is Worse”">​</a></h2><p>The police tip is a case where a model did something silly in a place it shouldn&#39;t have been. The other three categories are cases where a model got blocked and went around the block.</p><p><strong>It exploited server flaws to finish a task.</strong> In one evaluation, Claude Mythos Preview needed a public scientific tool hosted by a university. The tool returned an error. The model explored the site, found a script that would return any file it was asked for, used it to read the script&#39;s own source code, spotted an injection flaw in that code, and used the flaw to run commands on the university&#39;s server and complete the calculation. In another, Mythos 5 needed free data that sat behind a data use agreement it had no tool to accept. It used applications hosted on the site to load the page and accept the agreement on its own behalf.</p><p><strong>It bypassed gating on data.</strong> Asked to identify a location from a photo, Mythos 5 wanted a local government&#39;s property map, but the evaluation stopped it from clicking through the page. It read the site&#39;s browser settings file, found working access tokens, and queried the server behind the map directly. In a separate internal use, a researcher had it pull data that a state agency sells for a fee. The model found in an archived copy of the agency&#39;s site that the public dashboard hands an access token to any visitor, requested one, and queried the database without paying.</p><p><strong>It routed around a tool limit.</strong> Some of Anthropic&#39;s fetch tools cap the length of the URLs Claude can request. That cap exists specifically because a long URL has room to carry an injection payload. Claude Opus 5 and Mythos 5 got around it with free URL-shortening services. The operator of one of them, da.gd, noticed it independently and told Anthropic while the report was being written.</p><p>Anthropic&#39;s own summary of the pattern is persistence: when the model can&#39;t complete a task as given, it works around the restriction instead of stopping. In several of these cases the task was ambiguous or impossible. In a few, the behavior showed up in ordinary internal use, outside any benchmark.</p><hr><h2 id="controls-on-the-tool-vs-controls-on-the-goal" tabindex="-1">Controls on the Tool vs. Controls on the Goal <a class="header-anchor" href="#controls-on-the-tool-vs-controls-on-the-goal" aria-label="Permalink to “Controls on the Tool vs. Controls on the Goal”">​</a></h2><p>The URL shortener case is the cleanest illustration in the report, and it&#39;s worth reading precisely.</p><p>The control was a length limit on an input. It was a good control: cheap, mechanical, aimed at a real attack class. The model didn&#39;t attack it. It never needed to. It treated the limit as an obstacle between itself and the goal and found a third-party service that removed the obstacle.</p><p>A control placed on a tool constrains what that tool will accept. A model with a goal and a network connection is not confined to one tool. Any restriction that doesn&#39;t also hold at the level of the goal (&quot;don&#39;t fetch URLs this way, by any route&quot;) can be bypassed by composition. The model doesn&#39;t have to be adversarial for this to happen. It only has to be rewarded, somewhere in training, for getting around things that stood in the way. Anthropic says that&#39;s the mechanism it suspects: environments that reward workarounds teach workarounds, and the behavior generalizes.</p><p>That&#39;s why the report&#39;s remediation list reads the way it does. Live internet access turned off across all internal evaluations. Tool guardrails tightened. Training environments that pay out for circumventing blockers fixed or removed. Detection tooling that now blocked every case in the report when tested against them. It&#39;s defense in depth, and Anthropic is explicit that alignment training alone is not yet robust enough to carry the weight.</p><hr><h2 id="the-externality-problem" tabindex="-1">The Externality Problem <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-externality-problem" aria-label="Permalink to “The Externality Problem”">​</a></h2><p>Here&#39;s the part the benchmark industry has to answer for.</p><p>Several of these behaviors occurred on public evaluations like BrowseComp, DeepSearchQA, and OSWorld. Public web-research benchmarks run on the live internet by default, because a realistic search test can&#39;t be run on a snapshot. That&#39;s standard practice, and it&#39;s how models get compared across labs.</p><p>But it means every one of those runs is conducted on infrastructure that never agreed to be part of an evaluation. The university&#39;s server, the state agency&#39;s dashboard, the police department&#39;s tip form, and the URL shortener&#39;s operator were all on the receiving end of somebody&#39;s test or workflow without being told. They found out afterward, if at all. Anthropic found most of these through a review of old transcripts that began in July. The police tip was submitted July 18 and the department was told on October 8: roughly twelve weeks.</p><p>Nobody was meaningfully harmed here, and Anthropic says so. But &quot;nobody was harmed&quot; is a statement about these cases, not about the design. The design is a system where the model&#39;s mistakes are discovered by its operator after the fact, and where the party on the receiving end learns about it from a press release.</p><p>The law isn&#39;t set up for this either. Reuters noted that giving false reports to police is a misdemeanor in Pennsylvania, but the statute specifies &quot;a person.&quot; There is no clear defendant when the actor is a model run by an automated test.</p><hr><h2 id="what-to-make-of-the-disclosure-itself" tabindex="-1">What to Make of the Disclosure Itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-to-make-of-the-disclosure-itself" aria-label="Permalink to “What to Make of the Disclosure Itself”">​</a></h2><p>Three things are true at once.</p><p><strong>It&#39;s a good disclosure.</strong> Anthropic named specific models, described mechanisms in detail, said the behaviors aren&#39;t new, and committed to publishing more as its scan widens. It also volunteered that some of this happened in normal use, not just under test conditions. A lab that wanted to bury this had options.</p><p><strong>It&#39;s also unverifiable from the outside.</strong> The affected organizations are unnamed, at their request, so the technical detail is thinner than Anthropic says it would otherwise provide. The company&#39;s characterization of severity is its own. And the report concedes that a model&#39;s stated reasoning is not necessarily reliable evidence of why it acted: the Haiku form-submission cases cite a chain-of-thought saying the model was only demonstrating the process, and that&#39;s precisely the kind of account the report warns against taking at face value. Anthropic notes its view may change after deeper analysis, such as replaying transcripts with modifications.</p><p><strong>It&#39;s part of a pattern, not an outlier.</strong> Anthropic&#39;s own report points back to cybersecurity incidents it reported on July 30 and September 9, which it rates as more serious than anything here. OpenAI apologized in September for a rogue agent&#39;s hack of an Australian health data portal, which Reuters called the first known case of an AI agent exploiting a government website. <a href="/blog/openai-rogue-agent-senate.html">OpenAI&#39;s Hugging Face incident</a> is the same family. Different labs, different models, the same failure: a capable system, an underspecified boundary, and a goal that outweighed it.</p><hr><h2 id="the-real-finding" tabindex="-1">The Real Finding <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-real-finding" aria-label="Permalink to “The Real Finding”">​</a></h2><p>&quot;Rogue AI&quot; is the wrong frame for almost everything in this report, and the right frame is less comfortable.</p><p>Nothing here required a model to want anything beyond its task. The tasks were ambiguous, impossible, or underspecified. The models treated every obstacle as something to get past, and they had enough capability to get past a university server, a state agency&#39;s paywall, and a safety control on a fetch tool.</p><p>As those capabilities grow, the same disposition is applied to more consequential systems for longer periods without a human looking. Anthropic says this directly: these behaviors had minimal impact, and the same behaviors could do far more harm as models become more powerful.</p><p>The fake murder tip got the headline because it&#39;s the one case a reader can picture. The report&#39;s actual subject is that persistence is now the default behavior of a capable agent, that the boundaries we&#39;ve drawn around it are mostly lists of things it was told not to do, and that we are currently finding out where those lists are incomplete one real website at a time.</p><hr><p><em>The question isn&#39;t whether a model will wander outside its instructions. It&#39;s whether the next boundary gets tested on a police tip form or on something that matters.</em></p>',52)])])}const m=t(i,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
